Committed. Here's what happened.

## What I did

Blind round 81 — the fixture-shape census. Prediction written and mirrored to #738 before opening the file, with the blindfold's scope disclosed (the census half was already settled by grep; the bet was on the consequence half).

**The finding: three defects turned out to be one.** I captured four never-tested diff shapes as verbatim `git show` output from a scratch repo. Three of the four produced the identical symptom — `feat(): update code`, the literal empty parens:

| shape | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| pure rename | feat(): update code | feat(new): update code |
| binary change | feat(): update code | feat(img): update code |
| mode-only change | feat(): update code | feat(keep): update code |
| rename + content | feat(renamed2): add changes | unchanged (already correct) |

git emits no ---/+++ lines at all for those three, so the diff --git a/X b/Y header is their only mention of the file — and nothing read it. Same class as 26defce9, arriving from three more input shapes. One fix (diff_header_path + a pending header path flushed at the next header and at end of diff).

Verified rather than assumed: both +++ branches clear the pending path, which is what stops double-counting — checked against a real 4-file commit (git show --name-only agrees on 4) and against 26defce9, still refactor(mutants): remove code. Positive control run twice, before and after a restructure: neutering the helper fails exactly the census tests.

Filed, not absorbed: #829 (quoted headers — git quotes paths with special chars, my prefix doesn't match) and #830 (a path containing a literal " b/" is ambiguous; the parser refuses rather than inventing a path). CLAUDE.md is a reader surface; the issue queue is the scheduler surface.

## Grades: 1 hit / 1 miss

h1 hit — the pure rename, derivable from a mechanism I could state before reading anything.

h2 miss, cleanly, by my own pre-registered falsification condition. I asserted git emits --- a/x / +++ b/x before Binary files … differ. It does not. One git show would have settled it — a claim about an external tool's output format that I didn't check, which is a coin flip wearing a prior's clothes. That's the losing half of the derivable-vs-choice-dependent split running since round 73, and I walked straight into it.

## The rule

Before reading a mutation score for a function, check which input shapes its fixtures build. The score is bounded by them. That's a second ceiling, independent of the tool-side one reading #5 measured — that one is set by what the instrument can generate, this one by what the fixtures can ask. A function can sit at 0% survival with an entire branch never entered.

